WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.820
Short and sweet number 16. We presented many different solutions for leaving wine unattended with a non-Jew.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:15.580
The first solution was if the bottle of wine has a double seal, so once it has been double sealed, it may be left with a non-Jew unsupervised.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:27.420
Ramosha told us that even a single seal, but that is almost impossible to replicate, would be considered a double seal and would protect the wine from being left unsupervised with a non-Jew.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:42.760
We said that metal screw caps on a bottle would be considered a double seal because the moment that they are opened, the ring breaks off and the only way to reseal the bottle would be to bring it back to the factory, which is almost impossible, in which case it's also considered a double seal.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:56.980
A cork with a foil wrapper around it is also considered a double seal. The cork alone in the bottle is just one seal. Some people actually held that it was a double seal because the cork breaks when it's opened and therefore it's also impossible to replicate.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:05.920
Even those who did not agree held that a champagne cork would be considered a double seal because it is pretty much impossible to get back in once it has been taken out.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:14.300
If a person needed to create a double seal, they can put the bottle of wine in a plastic bag and tie the plastic bag very tightly around the bottle,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:20.040
put tape around the bag, which can tear very easily if it was tried to be removed,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.360
and sign both the tape and the bag. That would be an effective double seal around the bottle.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:34.500
Some opinions considered putting the cork so tightly in the bottle that it would need a tool to remove it as one seal.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.620
And we also discussed the question of these new inventions of the caps with the combination lock.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:46.660
So if it only has a three-digit code, there are those who say it is not a good double seal because it's possible to crack within a half an hour.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:57.700
However, even those who are Machmir held that a four-digit code would be considered double seal because it would be very difficult for the non-Jew to crack the code.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:04.800
And again, a four-digit code would for sure be a double seal. A three-digit code, according to some, is a double seal. According to some, it's not.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:16.180
If a person, B'dievet, left a bottle with a non-Jew with only one seal, the Rama told us, for the Ashkenazim, the bottle is Muter B'dievet.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:25.880
And the Aruch HaShulchan said that because non-Jews don't lie bait nowadays, it's even enough to have one seal on a bottle.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:35.000
If a situation came up where a bottle was left with a non-Jew, but the circumstance is that the non-Jew knows that they are not allowed to touch the wine,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:41.420
and on top of that they are afraid of getting caught touching the wine because they could lose their job, then the bottle would be permissible.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:03:01.180
We introduced also the solution of Yosef and Echnas. That's where we have the possibility for a Jew to show up at any time unexpectedly, and the non-Jew would be caught off guard if they were trying to touch the bottle and a Jew could come in at any time.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:15.780
So, in that situation, the non-Jew may be left with an open bottle of wine, again, assuming that they know that they're not allowed to touch it, and then they'd be afraid to do so because at any point a Jew can pop in without warning, that fear, that mirsos is enough to prevent them from doing so.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.260
However, there are four requirements that are necessary to rely on Yosef and Ichnas.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.440
The first one is that the non-Jew must think that the Jew can enter at any time.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:34.380
The second one is that when the Jew returns, the door must be unlocked, or at least if it's locked, they can see through the door clearly to the bottle of wine.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.200
The third requirement is that the non-Jew is not allowed to see the path that the Jew would take to get inside.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.740
If they can see it, we assume that they know, and they have ample warning to protect themselves.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.160
of wine. If a person was relying on Yetziv V'Nichas, and they

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:07.340
are lacking one of these requirements, and again, assuming that the non-Jew

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.340
is an idol worshiper or a Christian, or is a Christian, so then the bottle

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.180
and the barrel, if the bottle and barrel is open, so then the wine would be immediately

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.720
forbidden. We are concerned that they touched it and had

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.320
intent to libate. However, if the bottle or the barrel, which was left of the non-Jew, is closed

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.800
with a forgable seal, so then it's not forbidden immediately.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:49.840
If a person has live cameras pointed at the wine, and the non-Jew knows that they are not allowed to touch the wine, and they at least have a strong suspicion or know that you will be watching those cameras frequently, most posts can consider it a heter of Yetziv Adichnas, and it would protect the wine.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:57.520
wine. We lastly said that if a bottle of wine is hidden away in a place where it will not be able

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.120
to be found by a non-Jew, so then you could leave the non-Jew unsupervised in the house, and we are

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.700
not concerned that they have found the bottle and touched it as long as it is hidden very well.
